WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.200
In the central dogma, the process by which the DNA&nbsp;
is being turned into RNA is called transcription.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.520
Once you've made the messenger RNA, when it turned&nbsp;
into proteins, that process is called translation.&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.160
Proteins are complex polymer. The repeating&nbsp;
sub-units that are found in proteins are called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.400
amino acids and there are essentially 20 different&nbsp;
kinds of amino acids. The amino acids have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.520
amine part of the molecule, and they have an acid&nbsp;
part of the molecule, and a variable side chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.160
The variable side chain can consist of acidic&nbsp;
residues, basic residues, hydrophobic residues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.280
Based on whether they're charged, like&nbsp;
the acids or the bases, or uncharged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.480
or hydrophobic or hydrophilic, the different&nbsp;
amino acids, has slightly different properties.&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.640
Each amino acid can hooked together by the amino,&nbsp;
and the acid part of it and form a long chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.440
The side chains kind of hanging off of the&nbsp;
side. The polypeptide formed can then fold up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.000
sort of like a string of spaghetti, because&nbsp;
the polymer part of the chain is very flexible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.680
However, what they like to do is fold up into&nbsp;
a ball, so that the hydrophobic parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.240
molecule will hide away from the water and the&nbsp;
hydrophilic parts will interact with the water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.840
Some of the reactive parts, the acids and&nbsp;
the bases, are in pockets on the surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.840
These typically form what are&nbsp;
called the active sites of enzymes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.960
And because those are reactive acids&nbsp;
and bases, they can catalyze reactions.&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.200
For example, when you eat sugar, that&nbsp;
goes through a process called glycolysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.280
which involves a series of proteins that&nbsp;
are folded up into a particular shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.880
such that they recognize glucose. The sugar&nbsp;
binds to the enzyme, which is a protein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.640
in what is called a lock and key mechanism.&nbsp;
It fits in a very precise way, such that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.440
certain chemical moieties are in close contact&nbsp;
with certain amino acids that are reactive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.360
typically acids or bases. That allows the&nbsp;
enzyme to do a chemical reaction on the sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.560
so the sugar begins to be broken&nbsp;
down in a series of steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.880
About 15 steps that break down the sugar, allow&nbsp;
your body to use the pieces of the sugar to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:23.520
ATP that fuel chemical reaction of many biological&nbsp;
processes in the body. Some part of the sugar will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.400
be used to make the amino acids that went into&nbsp;
the protein, and some part will be sued to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.600
the nucleotides that go together to make the RNA&nbsp;
and to make the DNA, in the most efficient way.&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:41.040
Not all protein are enzymes. Some proteins have&nbsp;
a particular shape that lets them fit together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.040
and this forms the cytoskeleton, which&nbsp;
gives your cells the shape that they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.640
and allows your cells to fit together&nbsp;
to give your body the shape that it has.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.000
It's the three dimensional shape of the proteins&nbsp;
that allows them to carry out their functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.600
whether it's structural, or&nbsp;
metabolic, or energy conservation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.840
or information processing. That shape is caused&nbsp;
by the amino acids and their chemical properties.&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.640
But the trick that makes this work, they have&nbsp;
to be hooked together in the proper order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:15.520
one after another. So, a typical protein may have&nbsp;
from 100 to maybe 1000 different amino acids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.480
Where is that order of amino acids come from?&nbsp;
It comes from the RNA, and the RNA gets that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.240
from the DNA. So what the DNA code does is&nbsp;
that the bases in the DNA go together to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.520
the ribosome through the action of the messenger&nbsp;
RNA, what order to hook the amino acids together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.320
This is called the genetic code. So, in your&nbsp;
DNA, you've learned there are four bases, A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.840
T, G, and C. And the order at which those&nbsp;
are found in the DNA, three at a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.280
So, the order of amino acids that are put&nbsp;
together in the protein by the ribosome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

